From 0dde4f576b742e4ee64f4f574d8d5464966ef3e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 17:42:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ParaView exports: values written in single precision
 (parameter SinglePrecision)

The exports for ParaView (`ParaviewExportResults`,
`ParaviewExportIntegrationPointResultsAtNodes`) write the coordinates of
the nodes and the fields in double precision (`Float64`), compressed. For
a polycrystal of 38,400 grains whose displacement and stresses are written
at each of its 20 time steps, they would write about 670 GB.

The new optional parameter `SinglePrecision` (`false` by default) of both
post-processings writes these values in single precision (`Float32`, the
`BINARY32` format of MFEM), rounded to the nearest: the files take about
half the room. Without it, the files are the same as before. For a
polycrystal of 600 grains on one node (mm-opera-hpc), whose displacement
and von Mises stress are written in one collection at each of its 20 time
steps, the files take 4.4 GB against 7.5 GB, and are written in 226 s
against 255 s (the writing is dominated by the evaluation of the fields
element by element and by the compression, not by the size).

The writing of the files of `ParaviewExportResults` is timed by its own
profiling section, `ParaviewExportResults::Save`.

ParaviewExportResultsTest (new): the coordinates of the nodes and the
displacement are written in double precision by default and in single
precision with the parameter, at the initial time and at the end of a time
step, on one and two processes.
